<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lower Styria</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Štajerska</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> is the southern, Slovene-speaking part of the historical Duchy of Styria, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maribor">Maribor</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celje">Celje</a> and Ptuj, now in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>. Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands for centuries, it was exposed to Ottoman raids in the 15th and 16th centuries. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> it was separated from Austrian Styria and joined the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a> in 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lower-styria.jpg" alt="Lower Styria" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrej Jakobčič · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mariborsko_pohorje_panorama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
